  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMost of this activity should depart after 13Z or so with the \ninitial shortwave. After a lull, another round of likely more \nscattered showers and storms is expected during the late \nafternoon through around midnight or so with another shortwave \nin the flow. With a drier overall column by that point and \ncorrespondingly more subdued PWAT of 1.2 to 1.5 inches, the \nheavy rain threat should be a little lower. Nevertheless, that \ndrier air aloft will help tick up the threat of gusty to \nborderline severe winds, as 1500-2000 J/kg of surface-based CAPE\nand around 1000 J/kg of downdraft CAPE appear likely at least \ninto the early to middle evening hours.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe second round should end by 06Z tonight, and then a needed \nbreak still appears likely through Sunday and Sunday night. \nModel soundings continue to suggest a drier column and mid-level\ncapping that would prevent convective development. Dewpoints in\nthe mid and upper 60s, after today's readings in the 70s, \nshould lend a less sticky feel to the air. \n\nThe overall trough remains in place through much of next week. \nDaily showers and thunderstorm chances remain in the forecast \nfor Monday through at least Thursday, as several shortwave \nripples continue to ride through on the flow. Heavy rain \npotential will need continued scrutiny, as precipitable water \nvalues remain above-normal in a climatological sense. WPC will \nlikely place our region in a Slight (level 2 of 4) Risk for \nexcessive rainfall on that day.\n\nSevere weather chances show an uptick as well, particularly\nearlier in the week according to the long-range machine-learning\nconvective guidance. This makes sense, as this period shows the\nbest juxtaposition of instability, moderate deep-layer shear,\nand decent low-level flow. Some uncertainty remains among the\nvarious ensemble members that will be better resolved in time.",
